description:
`git diff src/doctor.rs` on this checkout shows uncommitted changes: `count_unflushed()` was made `pub` and a debug print was added directly inside `repair()`:

    // DEBUG: Verify clear_dirty worked
    let remaining_dirty = storage.list_dirty_issues()?.len();
    eprintln!("DEBUG: After repair + clear_dirty, remaining_dirty = {}", remaining_dirty);

This is mid-investigation instrumentation (someone was checking whether `storage.clear_dirty()`, called just above in repair() per the comment "create_issue_tx doesn't mark dirty, but we clear for safety", actually clears all dirty markers after a doctor --repair). It is uncommitted, untested, and prints unconditionally to stderr on every `bf doctor --repair` invocation.

Acceptance criteria:
- Determine whether clear_dirty() after repair() actually leaves 0 dirty issues (extend tests/doctor_repair_unflushed.rs or tests/test_dirty_repair.rs with an explicit assertion on the post-repair dirty count).
- If a real bug is found (dirty count > 0 after repair), fix it.
- Either remove the eprintln! entirely, or convert it to a `tracing::debug!` call (Cargo.toml already depends on tracing/tracing-subscriber) -- never a bare unconditional eprintln! in a shipped code path.
- Commit the resolution so `git status` on src/doctor.rs is clean.
